Now apply it to the wind moving through trees.

A camera observes leaves oscillating.

A microphone records changes in acoustic energy.

A tactile sensor measures variations in force.

Each measures something different, but their outputs may describe the same underlying process:

WIND → FORCE → DEFORMATION → OSCILLATION → LIGHT/SOUND CHANGES

That’s the shared semantic structure.

We can encode those relationships independently of the sensor, then use the modality-specific information to reconstruct the phenomenon in a new medium.

For example, translate the timing and amplitude of leaf movement into musical rhythm and dynamics. Or use recorded audio to estimate movement patterns in an animation.

Neither transformation has to resemble the original input. It must preserve whichever functional relationships matter to the task.

