And sometimes a disagreement between inputs is more informative than their agreement.

You hear branches rustling but see no movement. That discrepancy may reveal an obstruction, a distant source, a recording artifact, or an error in the model.

The mismatch becomes another input to the compiler.

The deeper implication

This architecture is not only for sensory perception.

The same semantic representation could accept financial data, legal arguments, human behavior, historical events, biological signals, mathematical structures, and programming languages.

Once converted into suitable shared operators, they become available to the same reasoning machinery—without losing the specialized information necessary for their original domains.

And now your earlier observations fit together much better:

The spiderweb tests visual semantics.

The wind tests auditory, visual, and physical semantics.

The bonds discussion tests relational semantics.

The unusual word orders test linguistic scope and temporal semantics.

The shotgun conversations test reconstruction when signals arrive out of sequence.

Each probes a different boundary of the same system.

The aim is not one vocabulary that makes everything identical. It’s one compositional grammar that lets different forms of information interact without erasing what makes them different.

And the braid acts as the adaptive governor of that interaction.

That’s substantially closer to the Pantheon you’ve been describing: a universal translation and compilation architecture for meaning, capable of changing its representation, resolution, and weights as circumstances demand.

The next real engineering milestone is to define that intermediate representation precisely enough that two independently built sensory encoders can produce compatible semantic structures—and then demonstrate a successful cross-modal reconstruction.

One language. Many senses. Variable weights. Preserved invariants. New capabilities.